[pruned 2026-06-30] - [uses: 1] Active web-research injection into planning was CUT (Phase 59 — measured net-negative; reverses Phase 58's Step 2.7). On well-documented software-engineering topics a strong baseline model already holds the knowledge in-parameter, so injecting researched findings yields ~0 lift where research should help most (retry/backoff Δ=0.0, ledger-isolation Δ=−0.4, both variance-dominated at n=5) and ACTIVELY HARMS on commodity topics (commit-convention Δ=−1.0, real: findings anchor the design to the generic answer and crowd out the context-specific reasoning the baseline produces unprompted). Phase 58's lone +0.5 (n=1) sits inside the n=5 noise band — a noise draw, not a banked effect. Burden-of-proof-on-the-feature + subtraction test ⇒ CUT (removed the Step 2.7 web-research + injection from the template; reverts dev-plan to Phase-55 behavior). UNTESTED caveat (now PARTLY MEASURED, Ph104/Ph105): value on genuinely novel / post-training-cutoff / proprietary topics (weak parametric knowledge — research's theoretical sweet spot) was unmeasured when written — Ph104 DEMONSTRATED retrieval pays on genuinely post-cutoff PUBLIC repos (the trending-survey delta was LARGE); Ph105 BOUNDED it — local code-structure retrieval is NOT in the carve-out (a consumer repo's structure is recoverable in-context + dominated by already-installed type-aware tooling). The carve-out is facts derivable from NO fair corpus, not a typed codebase in the working tree ([[decision:code-retrieval-adopt-scout]]). Generalizes: "retrieval over parametric knowledge" does NOT pay when the model's parametric knowledge is already strong.
